THIS WILL BE THE LAST PUBLIC RELEASE OF CASTLE DUNGEON FOR THE PARAGON/STARNET
BBS SOFTWARE. I have made the switch to DLG PRO due to lack of
professionalism on the part of both authors of Paragon and StarNet and the
fact that they seem to support their software only when they happen to feel
like it. I highly suggest any other disgruntled Paragon/StarNet owners to
make the switch to DLG PRO as it is one of the most amazing hunks of code I
have ever encountered. ALL FUTURE VERSIONS OF CASTLE DUNGEON WILL BE
WRITTEN TO RUN AS A DLG DOOR.

            Instructions on setting up Castle Dungeon version 1.05

    If you already have an earlier version of Blackmar's Dungeon, you will have
to restart your game as Castle Dungeon's dungeon files are not compatible with
the older version of the game and I haven't had the time to write a conversion
program. Any future versions of Castle Dungeon will include a conversion
program for older Castle Dungeon files if the new version isn't compatible with
them.    

1:  All files must go in dir Doors:Dungeon.  This means your door directory
    MUST be assigned as doors: and a directory inside it will be named 
    Dungeon.  This is usally most peoples mistake.

2:  A config file is now provided.  It contains one line with the number of
    moves per day.  

3:  All the text files for the monsters can be edited to any monster you
    want.  Rooms can also be edited but you must leave the doors in their
    appropriate places.

4:  Put the Map.txt up for download, or as a Bulletin (if you want).

Thats about it, I wrote these instruction's assuming you have set up other
doors game before, so I wont get into how to do that.

    For updates or bugs, call 
            Les's Place: The Mansion  (313) 391-6856, USR HST 14.4K (My BBS).
            Or you can contact me via USENET at Les@lppl.mi.org.

                                         Sincerely,
                                                Les Jenkins

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
History of Castle Dungeon. This has been updated and includes the original 
history for Blackmar's Dungeon so you may want to at least skim over it.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Originally, this door program was known as Blackmar's Dungeon and was written
by a fellow who only left his alias as any clue to his identity. It became a
very popular door for Paragon shortly after it's initial release. As far as I
know, the last version Blackmar himself put out was version 1.25 which is still
running on many systems today. Following is his history of the game up until
version 1.25.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Back around 1982 I used to play Avatar on the Plato system.  This is
a multiuser network, kinda like Genie or Compuserve but more educationally
based.  Many times there were 50 people or more in the dungeon.  Users
could not fight each other, but they could go in groups like real D&D.

After graduation, I no longer had an account on Plato.  I wrote a small
version on my TI99.  This version had a randomized map, everytime the
game was run.  It was not multiplayer and not a online game.

In the summer of 1990 I started writing a version for my Amiga, in BASIC,
and it worked with a small host that I also wrote in BASIC.  After getting
a small working version done, I showed my results to Broken Lips, who 
liked the game, and joined in the effort with ideas and programming.

Then Chris Timmerberg logged onto my BASIC host and tried the game.  He
also like the idea, and offered to translate the BASIC code to C code.

I accepted, since then it could be run as a Paragon Door, and since I 
didnt know C, the source code would enable me to learn.  

Chris converted the pre-beta in about 2 weeks, and had it pretty much bug
free.  At first I couldnt add anything, because I was lost in his code.  But
Broken Lips who also wanted to learn C made it alot easier for me.  In 
about 4 weeks I released the beta version.

Mr. Dos did alot of testing and suggestions.  Also Joe at his work added
the moves per day function.

There has been several versions since, and the game keeps growing.  The 
source is twice as big as the 1st pre-Beta C version was.  The game is now
played in several countries. 

I now work on the program mostly by myself, with occasional help from Broken
Lips.  The most important thing about Blackmar's Dungeon is I learned C
while writing it.  Ive never had any instruction in programming, I just do
it as a hobby.

Look for Phil Hypos "Knight Realm" from Italy.  A game similar to mine, with
some D&D stuff that I left out of Blackmar's Dungeon.

I really appreciate the comments and bug reports that I have gotten.  
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Chris Timmerberg released Dungeon 2.0 along with it's source code a few months
back and I haven't seen an update since then either. He said in his doc file
that he would be making a version 3.0 with a new display system. The fact that
he released the source code to version 2.0 I took as a sign that he MIGHT
release version 3.0 if he ever got around to writing it and decided I would
pick up the reigns were he and Blackmar had left off. This of course means
there may be more than one version of this game floating around before we are
done. ;-)  First off, I want to thank both Blackmar, for giving us such a
wonderful game to begin with, and Chris, for releasing a nicely updated version
along with the source code. Castle Dungeon 1.0 would not exist if not for the
wonderful code these two authors put together.
        Next, I want to thank the man who has co-written the changes to Castle
Dungeon along with me. Namely Dead God (as he is known on my system) without
whom, I wouldn't be learning Amiga C as well as I am.
        Please see the file CHANGES.TXT for a list of changes from Blackmar's
1.25 and Dungeon 2.00 and for a list of future additions to the game. Please
send all bug reports/complaints/complements/etc to my BBS either at the number
provided above or via Netmail. The latest version of Castle Dungeon can always
be found on my BBS.

